  <!-- The Formula -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-4">The Formula & Mathematical Methodology</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Converting square feet to square yards requires a simple division by 9, but calculating broadloom material orders requires factoring in roll widths and seam allowances:</p>
    
    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-4 mb-5 font-mono text-sm text-gray-800 space-y-2 border border-gray-200">
      <p>1. Net Floor Area (sq ft) = (Length × Width × Rooms) − Subtracted Obstacles</p>
      <p>2. Net Square Yards (sq yd) = Net Area (sq ft) / 9</p>
      <p>3. Gross Square Yards to Order = Net Square Yards × (1 + Waste Factor)</p>
      <p>4. Linear Feet of 12-ft Roll = (Gross Square Yards × 9) / 12</p>
      <p>5. Price Conversion: $/sq ft = ($/sq yd) / 9  |  $/sq yd = ($/sq ft) × 9</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why Divide by 9?</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-3">
      One linear yard equals 3 linear feet. Therefore, a square yard is a square measuring 3 feet on each side:
      <strong>3 ft × 3 ft = 9 square feet</strong>. Dividing square feet by 9 converts the area directly into square yards.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">Worked Example: 13' × 18' Master Bedroom</h3>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-sm text-gray-600 space-y-1 mb-4">
      <li><strong>Net Area:</strong> 13 ft × 18 ft = 234 sq ft</li>
      <li><strong>Net Square Yards:</strong> 234 sq ft ÷ 9 = 26.0 sq yd</li>
      <li><strong>With 10% Waste:</strong> 26.0 × 1.10 = <strong>28.6 sq yd</strong> to order</li>
      <li><strong>Gross Sq Ft:</strong> 28.6 × 9 = 257.4 sq ft</li>
      <li><strong>12-Foot Roll Cut:</strong> 257.4 sq ft ÷ 12 ft roll width = <strong>21.5 linear feet</strong> of 12' roll</li>
      <li><strong>Price Comparison:</strong> At $36/sq yd, the room material is $1,029.60 (which equals $4.00/sq ft)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

