  <!-- Frequently Asked Questions -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-5">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="space-y-5">
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How do I convert square feet to square yards for carpet?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">To convert square feet to square yards, divide your total square footage by 9 (because 1 yard = 3 feet, and 3 × 3 = 9 square feet per square yard). For example, a 180 sq ft room equals 180 ÷ 9 = 20 square yards. Add 10% waste for cutoffs and seams, bringing the total to purchase to 22 square yards.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How many square yards of carpet do I need for a 12x12 room?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">A 12x12 room is 144 square feet. 144 ÷ 9 = 16 net square yards. Because standard broadloom carpet is manufactured on 12-foot wide rolls, a 12x12 room fits a single 12-foot roll cut seamlessly. Adding 10% waste for trimming and wall squareness brings the recommended order to 17.6 square yards (or approximately 13.5 to 14 linear feet of a 12-foot roll).</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How do I convert carpet cost per square yard to square foot?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">To convert carpet price from square yards to square feet, divide the price per square yard by 9. For example, carpet priced at $36 per square yard costs $4.00 per square foot ($36 ÷ 9 = $4.00). Conversely, multiply a square foot price by 9 to get the square yard rate ($5.50/sq ft × 9 = $49.50/sq yd).</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Why is carpet measured in square yards instead of square feet?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Carpet is historically manufactured and sold on 12-foot or 15-foot wide rolls. Measuring in square yards is the industry standard for pricing and cutting these large roll widths.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How do broadloom roll widths (12 ft vs 15 ft) affect carpet seams?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Standard carpet rolls are 12 feet wide (with select styles available in 15 feet). If your room is 14 feet wide, a 12-foot roll cannot cover it in a single pass and requires a cross-seam. The installer must order a longer cut and turn a 2-foot strip to fill the gap. Crucially, pile direction must match across the seam, or the seam will appear discolored under room lighting.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Is padding always necessary and ordered in the same quantity?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Yes, for traditional broadloom carpet. Padding provides cushioning, extends the life of the carpet by absorbing foot traffic, and offers sound insulation. Padding uses the exact same square yardage as carpet.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

